startup.nvim: guard the deferred cursor restore against buffer switches

The display() function defers an unconditional
`vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, { 2, 2 })` by 1ms (init.lua:565-571).
Window/buffer 0 means "whatever is current when the timer fires". A
keypress in that window can open a picker (e.g. Telescope find_files)
first, and the deferred set then lands in a 1-line prompt buffer and
fails with "Invalid cursor line: out of range".

Guard the callback: only restore the cursor while the startup buffer is
still current and the target line exists. This mirrors the guard
upstream already applies to the CursorMoved path in
startup.utils.reposition_cursor (utils.lua:469), which uses
`vim.o.filetype ~= "startup"`. Also capture startup.buffer_nr before the
defer, so a fast keypress cannot poison it with the picker's buffer id.
--- a/lua/startup/init.lua
+++ b/lua/startup/init.lua
@@ -562,12 +562,16 @@
         autocmd BufHidden * lua require"startup".remove_buffer(vim.fn.getbufinfo()[1])
         autocmd BufLeave * lua require"startup".remove_buffer(vim.fn.getbufinfo()[1])
     ]])
+    startup.buffer_nr = vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
     vim.defer_fn(function()
-        vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, {
-            2,
-            2,
-        })
-        startup.buffer_nr = vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
+        -- A keypress may open a picker (e.g. Telescope) in the 1ms before
+        -- this callback runs; only restore the cursor while still on the
+        -- startup buffer, and only when the target line exists. Mirrors the
+        -- filetype guard upstream uses in reposition_cursor (utils.lua:469).
+        if vim.o.filetype ~= "startup" or vim.api.nvim_buf_line_count(0) < 2 then
+            return
+        end
+        vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, { 2, 2 })
     end, 1)
     if force then
         startup.buffer_nr = vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
